From: Julian Cooper <115541810+Juliansgith@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:40:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 31/52] Agent: per-step timing breakdown and load-phase marks

Measuring where a training game's wall time goes needs more than the
step report's sim/observe/act totals.

- The step timing adds simCpuMs (sim-thread CPU since the last reply, as
  opposed to wall time, which includes waiting for a CPU), observeLuaMs
  (AgentBridge_Observe alone; observeMs - observeLuaMs is the JSON
  encoding), obsBytes and luaKB (the sim Lua heap).
- AgentBridge_Lap([name]) lets the bridge script time its own sections;
  the totals come back per step as timing.laps.
- AGENT_LoadMark(phase) records loading milestones in milliseconds since
  the process was created (begin_session, preload, rules_loaded,
  session_lua, map_loaded, loaded, session/driver created, sim_create,
  sim_created, first_sync, game_ui, first_beat, hello); the hello message
  reports them as "load". Hooks in WLD_BeginSession, the WLD frame
  actions, the scenario loader and CSimDriver::ThreadCreateSim; inert
  without /agentbridge.

(cherry picked from commit fb8b4764b9a30c28ce500c9d6d1b87a77d0d3a90)

Ported onto 8977bb9d: the driver and sim are now scoped_ptrs (sSimDriver.reset / mSim.reset in a try), so the load marks sit around upstream's reset calls instead of the old manual delete sequence.

